.TH PUMP-FAST 1 "September 2026" "Data Pump 001_00" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
pump-fast \- transfer text and files with the Fast modem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pump fast-info
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B pump fast-tx
(\fB\-\-text\fR \fItext\fR | \fB\-\-input\fR \fIfile\fR)
(\fB\-\-output\fR \fIwav\fR | \fB\-\-device\fR \fIdevice\fR)
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B pump fast-rx
.BI \-\-input " wav"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B pump fast-listen
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are subcommands of
.BR pump (1).
Fast transfers text and files through audio hardware or WAV files, using its
own QAM/LDPC or classic APSK format. Text and files are compressed with XZ.
The receiver reports status and metadata; use
.B \-\-save
to write completed content.
.PP
Both ends must select matching profile, waveform, coding and encryption
settings, and the same key material when encryption is enabled.
There is no negotiation, automatic fallback or retransmission.
Output channel and amplitude are local choices.
.PP
Reception completes only after consecutive, fully scored absent symbols or
OFDM blocks cover six seconds. EOF, cancellation, successful error correction
and integrity checks do not end reception. Generated WAV files include at
least 6.25 seconds of silence, extended when complete symbol or OFDM-block
observations require it. Preserve this tail when recording or editing audio.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B fast-info
Print the selected local settings and calculated rates as JSON.
Does not open audio hardware or load a keyfile.
.TP
.B fast-tx
Transmit exactly one text argument or file. Select exactly one destination:
a new WAV file or an audio device. Live progress is written to standard error
unless
.B \-\-json
is selected.
.TP
.B fast-rx
Receive one transfer from a WAV file. The recording must contain the
physical-end silence and use 16-bit PCM, one or two channels, and a sample
rate from 44100 through 192000 Hz (48000 Hz for OFDM).
No file is saved unless
.B \-\-save
is given and reception completes successfully.
.TP
.B fast-listen
Listen for one transfer from an audio device, then exit.
The default device is
.BR default .
Interrupting the command cancels reception.
.SH OPTIONS
Options follow the subcommand. Values are separate arguments; duplicate
options are rejected.
.TP
.BI \-\-text " text"
Transmit text, including empty text, with
.BR fast-tx .
The limit is 32768 source bytes, counting UTF-8 bytes.
.TP
.BI \-\-input " file"
Read a source file for
.BR fast-tx ,
or a WAV recording for
.BR fast-rx .
File transfers retain the source basename as a suggested filename.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " wav"
Write the transmitted waveform to a new mono 16-bit PCM WAV file.
Existing files are never overwritten. Classic RIFF limits output to 4 GiB.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " device"
Use this audio device for live transmission or reception. See
.B pump devices
for available devices. Live transmission requires this option explicitly.
.TP
.BI \-\-save " file"
Save exact completed receive bytes to a new file. Existing files are never
overwritten. For attachments, the saved bytes exclude the attachment prefix.
The received filename never selects the destination path.
.TP
.BI \-\-seconds " n"
Cancel live transmission or listening after
.I n
whole seconds. Zero, the default, sets no limit. Cancellation does not
complete a pending reception.
.TP
.BI \-\-estimate\-bytes " n"
For
.BR fast-info ,
estimate airtime for
.I n
encoded source bytes, including framing and end silence. This size-only
estimate does not read a file or measure compression.
.TP
.BI \-\-quota\-mb " n"
Set the local source-storage quota in MiB, from 1 to 256. Default: 256.
This is not a total process-memory limit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print result metadata as JSON.
.B fast-info
always prints JSON. Receive results distinguish completion, physical
completion, encryption and authentication; they do not contain source text.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show Fast command help and exit.
.SS Profiles and coding
Defaults below are the reference profiles selected without
.BR \-\-expected\-snr .
Use
.B fast-info
to inspect a fully resolved configuration.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " name"
Select
.BR wire " (default), " ssb ,
.BR fm ,
.BR acoustic " or " acoustic-short .
Wire is an audio-cable profile; SSB and FM use voice-band audio.
Acoustic profiles are for speakers and microphones; acoustic-short uses
shorter coding blocks and training. Radio profiles do not control a radio
or provide PTT.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " name"
Select
.BR capacity " (default) or " classic .
Capacity uses QAM and LDPC; modeled short-acoustic presets may use a compact
convolutional code. Classic uses QPSK/APSK and convolutional coding.
.TP
.BI \-\-expected\-snr " db"
Resolve a modeled capacity preset from the assumed SNR over the original
channel bandwidth: 25..65 dB over 18 kHz for wire, \-27..13 dB over 17.5 kHz
for acoustic profiles, and \-20..20 dB over 2.4 kHz for SSB/FM.
Weak presets narrow the bandwidth and may switch acoustic OFDM to a single
carrier. SNR is assumed, not measured or negotiated. Explicit waveform and
coding options override the preset regardless of argument order.
.TP
.BI \-\-qam " order"
Select a capacity constellation: 4, 16, 64, 256, 1024, 4096, 16384, 65536,
262144, 1048576 or 4194304.
Defaults: wire 4194304, SSB/FM 64, acoustic profiles 16.
.TP
.BI \-\-apsk " order"
Select classic format and constellation 4, 16, 64 or 256.
Conflicts with
.B \-\-qam
or an explicit capacity format. Classic defaults: wire 256, SSB 16,
FM and acoustic profiles 4.
.TP
.BI \-\-code\-rate " rate"
Capacity rates: 1/2, 2/3, 3/4, 7/9, 8/9 or 9/10.
Short acoustic coding permits a subset depending on the selected preset.
Classic rates: 1/2, 3/4 or 7/8.
Defaults: capacity wire 8/9, classic wire 7/8, other profiles 3/4.
.TP
.BI \-\-rs " mode"
Select outer Reed\-Solomon coding.
Capacity requires
.BR 0.3% .
Classic accepts
.BR robust " or " high-rate ;
its defaults are high-rate for wire and robust for other profiles.
.TP
.BI \-\-interleave " n"
Set coding depth: 1..16 for capacity, 1..64 for classic.
Capacity defaults: wire/SSB/FM 4, acoustic 8, acoustic-short 1.
Classic defaults: wire 62, SSB/FM 16, acoustic profiles 5.
.SS Audio and waveform
.TP
.BI \-\-sample\-rate " hz"
Set processing audio rate, 44100..192000 Hz. Default: 48000.
OFDM requires 48000 Hz. Single-carrier peers may use different local
sample rates while retaining matching waveform settings.
.B fast-rx
uses the recording's sample rate.
.TP
.BI \-\-amplitude " n"
Set waveform amplitude, greater than zero and at most 0.8.
Capacity defaults: wire/SSB/FM 0.30, acoustic profiles 0.40.
Classic defaults: wire/acoustic profiles 0.35, SSB/FM 0.50.
.TP
.B \-\-mono
Send on the left output only. This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-right\-mono
Send on the right output only.
.TP
.B \-\-stereo
Send the same waveform on both outputs. The three routing options conflict.
.PP
For stereo WAV input,
.B fast-rx
uses these options to select the left channel, right channel, or their average,
respectively. WAV output is always mono.
.PP
The following options apply only to single-carrier waveforms.
The selected band must fit the processing audio rate.
.TP
.BI \-\-symbol\-rate " hz"
Set symbols per second; fractional values are accepted.
Capacity defaults: wire 18000/1.02, SSB/FM 2400/1.10.
Classic defaults: wire 15000, SSB/FM 2000, acoustic profiles 500.
.TP
.BI \-\-carrier " hz"
Set carrier frequency. Defaults: wire 9300, SSB/FM 1500,
classic acoustic profiles 1800.
.TP
.BI \-\-rolloff " n"
Set root-raised-cosine rolloff: 0.02..0.5 for capacity, 0.1..0.5 for classic.
Defaults: capacity wire 0.02, capacity SSB/FM 0.10, classic 0.20.
.TP
.BI \-\-marker\-spacing " n"
Set capacity intervals per full marker, 1..16.
Defaults: wire 16, SSB/FM 4.
.TP
.BI \-\-pilot\-spacing " n"
Set capacity data symbols per pilot group, 16..1024.
Defaults: wire 256, SSB/FM 64.
.PP
The following options apply only to acoustic capacity profiles using OFDM.
.TP
.BI \-\-ofdm\-fft " n"
Set FFT size, a power of two from 2048 to 32768.
Defaults: acoustic 32768, acoustic-short 8192.
.TP
.BI \-\-ofdm\-prefix " samples"
Set cyclic-prefix length, from 256 samples through the FFT size.
Defaults: acoustic 4096, acoustic-short 512.
.TP
.BI \-\-ofdm\-pilots " n"
Set pilot stride, 2..32. Default: 16.
.TP
.BI \-\-ofdm\-low " hz"
.TQ
.BI \-\-ofdm\-high " hz"
Set the occupied band edges. Defaults: 500 and 18000 Hz.
The band must lie within 100..20000 Hz, span at least 1000 Hz,
and contain at least 512 active frequency bins.
.SS Encryption
Encryption is off unless
.B \-\-keyfile
is supplied. Plaintext checksums detect corruption but do not authenticate.
.TP
.BI \-\-keyfile " file"
Enable encryption using an existing Data Pump symmetric keyfile.
Both peers must use the same key. See
.B pump keygen
for key creation.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-name " name"
Select a named key. The default is the first key in the file.
.TP
.BI \-\-pad " path"
Supply the external pad for a pad-backed keyfile.
.B \-\-key\-name
and
.B \-\-pad
require a keyfile unless
.B \-\-no\-encryption
is given.
.TP
.B \-\-encrypt
Require encryption and an explicit
.BR \-\-keyfile .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-encryption
Select plaintext and ignore all keyfile options. Conflicts with
.BR \-\-encrypt .
.SH EXAMPLES
Write a text transfer to a WAV file, then receive and save it:
.PP
.nf
pump fast-tx \-\-text 'Hello from Fast' \-\-output message.wav
pump fast-rx \-\-input message.wav \-\-save message.txt
.fi
.PP
Use the same modeled acoustic settings on both machines; start the listener
before transmitting:
.PP
.nf
pump fast-listen \-\-profile acoustic-short \-\-expected\-snr \-6 \e
    \-\-save received.bin
pump fast-tx \-\-profile acoustic-short \-\-expected\-snr \-6 \e
    \-\-input source.bin \-\-device default
.fi
.PP
Inspect a classic profile:
.PP
.nf
pump fast-info \-\-format classic \-\-apsk 256 \-\-code\-rate 7/8
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Help or information displayed, transmission succeeded, or reception completed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments, input/output failure, cancelled live operation,
or incomplete or failed reception.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pump (1),
.BR datapump-gui (1)
